{{also|nickel-and-dime}}
==English==
{{was wotd|2007|November|21}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|US}} {{IPA|en|/ˌnɪkəl ən ˈdaɪm/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-nickel and dime.ogg|Audio (US)}}
* {{audio|en|en-au-nickel and dime.ogg|Audio (AU)}}

===Alternative forms===
* [[nickel-and-dime]]

===Etymology 1===
From the names of two [[US]] [[coins]] of small value.

====Adjective====
{{head|en|adjectives}}

# {{lb|en|US|idiomatic|colloquial}} [[small-time|Small time]]; operating on a small [[scale]]; involving small amounts of [[money]]; [[petty]] or [[cheap]].
#: ''I bought my new ride from some '''nickel and dime''' used-car salesman.''
#: ''Don't waste your time with that; their operations are '''nickel and dime'''.''

====Verb====
{{en-verb|pres_3sg2=nickels and dimes|pres_ptc2=nickeling and diming|pres_ptc3=nickelling and diming|past2=nickeled and dimed|past3=nickelled and dimed}}

# {{lb|en|US|idiomatic|colloquial}} To charge, or be charged, several unexpected small amounts of [[money]], often in the form of [[fee]]s, taxes, or related expenses to a venture, which when taken as a whole add up to a significant unexpected cost.
#: ''I got '''nickel and dimed''' to death by the phone company's sneaky extra charges.''
#: ''It seems like a great offer, but they will just '''nickel and dime''' you until you've spent more than retail anyway.''
# {{lb|en|US|idiomatic|colloquial|figuratively}} To [[wear down]] in small increments; to [[quibble]] or [[obsess]] [[endlessly]] with (someone) over [[trifle]]s.

===See also===
* {{l|en|fleece}}

===Etymology 2===
From prison or criminal slang

===Noun===
{{head|en|noun}}

# {{lb|en|US|slang}} Fifteen years.

===Etymology 3===
After retail stores where “everything costs a nickel or a dime”
 
====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|US|and|Canada}} A [[retail]] store selling [[inexpensive]] items, especially one in which all items have a [[price]] near a [[nickle]] (US five cents) or a [[dime]] (US ten cents)
#: {{syn|en|five and ten|five and dime}}

===See also===
* [[dollar store]]

